From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nate Isern <nate@oxy.so>
Date: Sat, 9 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Offer Astro's Bloom presets in the colour picker, and let them
 paint

The "Customize Astro" side panel's colour picker offered Chromium's thirteen
GM3 colours and wrote them to `browser.theme.user_color2`. Astro's native UI
is painted by chrome/browser/oxy/ui/astro_color_mixer.cc from
`astro.theme.preset`, and the two do not meet: `SetUserColorAndBrowserColor-
Variant` REMOVES a theme supplier (ClearThemeData -> SwapThemeSupplier(nullptr))
and the seed lands in ColorProviderKey::user_color, a different field from
`custom_theme`, which is the only one the Astro mixer switches on. So the
picker moved the sys palette and the panel's own surfaces while the toolbar,
tab strip, omnibox and menus stayed on whatever preset was stored -- a control
that moves and changes the wrong thing.

WHAT THIS CHANGES

  GetChromeColors  builds its list from the Bloom presets this build OFFERS
                   (astro::IsColorPresetOffered over the generated table in
                   chrome/browser/oxy/ui/astro_color_tokens.h) instead of
                   kDynamicCustomizeChromeColors, which is now unreferenced
                   here along with ui::GeneratePalette.
  SetSeedColor     maps the clicked swatch back to an offered preset and
                   stores it through AstroThemeService::SetColorPreset.
  UpdateTheme      reports the ACTIVE preset's identity, so the check mark
                   follows astro.theme.preset however it was changed.

OFFERED, NOT ALL EIGHTEEN

Bloom gates three of its presets and only DECLARES the gate; enforcing it is
the consuming app's job. Astro offers what nobody gates, plus its own default
preset -- `oxy` is gated to Oxy's brand and this browser is Oxy's. `faircoin`
is another organisation's reserved brand and `mono` is sold with a
subscription, so neither is Astro's to hand out. Sixteen of eighteen, the same
palette webui/app's appearance section offers.

The first version of this patch offered all eighteen, because the gates reached
C++ as COMMENTS and a comment cannot be filtered on. They are data now
(kColorPresetGates), the rule that reads them is astro::IsColorPresetOffered in
chrome/browser/oxy/astro_theme_service.h, and
tools/tests/cases/offered-presets-match-across-the-boundary.sh compares the two
surfaces. AstroThemeService::SetColorPreset refuses a gated preset outright, so
the filter here is the palette and not the enforcement.

Upstream's "Grey" swatch writes Bloom's `gray`, not `mono`. `mono` is the
closer match by name and was the first choice; it is also the premium-gated
one, and a filtered palette beside a setter that still writes a gated preset is
the same defect with a smaller blast radius. Both that and the Default swatch's
preset are static_asserted to be offered.

Each swatch renders three colours and they are the three the preset actually
paints the browser with, taken straight from the mixer's own choices: the
circle is `surface` (the toolbar), the bottom-left quarter is `primary` (the
brand colour), the bottom-right quarter is `muted` (the frame and the inactive
tabs). The `seed` field is never painted -- the page uses it only to decide
which swatch is checked -- so it is an identity, and it is the light-scheme
`primary`: distinct across all eighteen presets (asserted at compile time,
because that is a property of a generated table) and deliberately independent
of dark mode, so toggling the scheme cannot desynchronise the page's copy from
the browser's.

THE SEED IS STILL WRITTEN, ON PURPOSE

SetSeedColor writes BOTH astro.theme.preset AND upstream's GM3 seed, set to
the preset's own Bloom `primary`. The mixer sets roughly thirty ColorIds; it
sets no ui::kColorSys* token, so everything derived from those -- this side
panel's own surfaces, the WebUI pages, the profile chooser -- would otherwise
keep a palette unrelated to the preset. Handing Chromium the same source
colour makes it generate its Material palette from the preset instead, which
is a better answer than hand-mapping forty sys tokens in the mixer, and it
leaves the mixer's own ColorIds untouched for the reason in the first
paragraph. Extending the mixer to the sys tokens stays with
https://github.com/OxyHQ/Astro/issues/24.

WHICH SURFACES MOVE

This handler is shared, so all of its consumers change together, which is the
intent -- all of Astro uses Bloom:

  * the Customize Astro side panel (customize_chrome_ui.cc);
  * astro://settings' appearance picker (settings_ui.cc);
  * chrome://profile-customization, the dialog shown when a profile is created
    (profile_customization_ui.cc).

WHAT IS LEFT

The hue slider behind the custom-colour circle asks for an arbitrary seed.
Astro's theme model has eighteen presets and nothing to answer that with, so
SetSeedColor refuses the request and logs it; the control is inert. Snapping
an arbitrary hue to the nearest preset would be this file deciding what
"custom colour" means in Astro, which is #24's decision, and removing the
control is a frontend change to a shared cr_component.

chrome_colors::GetDynamicColorId returns kOtherDynamicColorId for every Astro
seed, so the NTP colour-choice UMA histogram records "other". That is a
metrics bucket, not behaviour, and it is left alone.

THE BUILD EDGE IS PART OF THIS PATCH

`//chrome/browser/ui` gains a dependency on `//chrome/browser/oxy:theming`,
which it already had transitively through `//chrome/browser/ui/color:mixers`
(patch 061) -- so this introduces no cycle, and `gn gen` was re-run to confirm
it. `:theming` deliberately depends on nothing under //chrome/browser/ui,
which is what makes the edge safe in this direction.

Owned by https://github.com/OxyHQ/Astro/issues/24.
